No. You don't "connect" Python with Linux.

This is a very common confusion. Let me explain.

Think of your setup like this:
Your Laptop
│
├── Windows 11
│      ├── Python (Windows)
│      ├── VS Code (Windows)
│      └── Git (Windows)
│
└── VirtualBox
       │
       └── Ubuntu Linux
              ├── Python (Linux)
              ├── Git (Linux)
              └── ACTDRS Project

Notice something important:

Windows has its own Python installation.
Ubuntu has its own Python installation.

They are completely separate.

Why?

Your ACTDRS project is a Linux-based cybersecurity system.

It needs to use Linux features such as:

/var/log
systemctl
journalctl
Linux processes
Linux services
Linux firewall (UFW)
Linux permissions

The Windows Python installation cannot directly work with those Linux components.

Do I need to install Python again?

First, check whether Python is already installed in Ubuntu.

Open the Ubuntu Terminal and type:

python3 --version
If you get something like:
Python 3.12.3

✅ Great! You do not need to install Python again.

Just install pip:

sudo apt install python3-pip
If you get:
Command 'python3' not found

Then install Python:

sudo apt install python3
Do I need to connect Windows Python with Ubuntu?

No.

The Ubuntu Python interpreter will run your ACTDRS project.

The Windows Python installation is unrelated to this project.

My recommendation

Since ACTDRS is a Linux project, do everything inside Ubuntu:

✅ Write the code in Ubuntu.
✅ Run Python in Ubuntu.
✅ Install libraries with pip3 in Ubuntu.
✅ Test everything in Ubuntu.

This avoids compatibility problems later.

